An ambient generation criterion for F004
======================================

Status and attribution
----------------------

This is a static mathematical note, not an execution verdict or a census
adoption. It makes no claim about an Original case. The subgroup
classification and the exceptional group element orders are borrowed.
The proposed certificate applies those results to a small list of actual
matrix words. A scalar commutant alone does not establish irreducibility.

The original classification is P. B. Kleidman, *The maximal subgroups of
the Chevalley groups G2(q) with q odd, the Ree groups 2G2(q), and their
automorphism groups*, Journal of Algebra 117 (1988), 30--71,
https://doi.org/10.1016/0021-8693(88)90239-6 . Its full publisher text was
not accessible in this review. A table of the families, explicitly
attributed to that classification, is Table 2 in the author-hosted paper
by H. P. Tong-Viet and T. P. Wakefield, *On Huppert's conjecture for
G2(q), q >= 7*, https://www.personal.kent.edu/~htongvie/Publications/G2.pdf .
The argument below imports the stated classification cover; it does not
claim to reprove it or to have checked Kleidman's full original proof.

Conditional criterion
---------------------

Let G be the intended G2(11), acting in its faithful natural seven
dimensional representation over F_11. Let H <= G. Import the following
specialization of the maximal subgroup classification: each maximal
subgroup is either reducible in this representation or belongs to one
of the abstract families

  PGL2(11), 2^3.L3(2), G2(2), J1, PSL2(8), PSL2(13).

The final two families may be retained in this conservative cover even
though their field conditions exclude q=11. No exact conjugacy class
count is required. The geometric families are the two parabolics,
SL3(11):2, SU3(11):2, and (SL2(11) o SL2(11)).2. We explicitly import their
natural module restrictions as a further embedding premise: they preserve,
respectively, a proper flag, a line, a line, and the decomposition of
dimensions 3+4. The abstract family table alone does not establish this
embedding premise. There are no proper subfield or Ree families at q=11.

Assume that (i) the F_11 span of some actual words in H is all M_7(F_11),
and (ii) an actual element of H has order 24 or 44. Then H=G.

Proof
-----

If H were proper, it would lie in a maximal subgroup M. A reducible M
preserves a subspace W with 0 < dim(W) < 7. Every matrix in the span of
H preserves W, whereas M_7(F_11) does not. This contradicts (i).

The remaining abstract types have no element of order 24:

* In PGL2(11), a nontrivial element is unipotent of order 11, or is
  semisimple with order dividing 10 or 12. This follows by taking the
  ratio of the two eigenvalues over F_11 or its quadratic extension.
* In 2^3.L3(2), an element of order 24 would have image of order 12 or
  24 in L3(2), since its cyclic subgroup meets the elementary abelian
  kernel in a group of order at most two. Neither order occurs in
  L3(2): its element orders are 1,2,3,4,7.
* G2(2)=U3(3):2 has orders 1,2,3,4,6,7,8,12. This is borrowed from the
  complete sixteen class labels in the author-maintained ATLAS,
  https://brauer.maths.qmul.ac.uk/Atlas/exc/G22/ . The incomplete words
  in that page are not used as supplied certificates.
* J1 has orders 1,2,3,5,6,7,10,11,15,19, as recorded by its fifteen
  classes in https://brauer.maths.qmul.ac.uk/Atlas/spor/J1/ .
* PSL2(8) has orders 1,2,3,7,9, by the same eigenvalue argument for
  two dimensional projective groups. PSL2(13) has group order 1092,
  which is not divisible by 24.

They also have no element of order 44. The orders of 2^3.L3(2), G2(2),
PSL2(8), and PSL2(13) are not divisible by 11, whereas 44 is. The preceding
PGL2(11) order description excludes 44, and the J1 class list excludes it
as well. Thus either choice in (ii) excludes every remaining type, so
H=G. This conclusion uses the imported classification and geometric
representation statements explicitly.

The field exclusions, if desired, are given in Table 4.1 of D. A.
Craven, *An Ennola duality for subgroups of groups of Lie type*,
Monatshefte fuer Mathematik 199 (2022), 785--799,
https://doi.org/10.1007/s00605-022-01676-3 ; author preprint:
https://pure-oai.bham.ac.uk/ws/portalfiles/portal/154691077/ennoladuality.pdf .
That table requires 11 = +/-1 modulo 9 for PSL2(8), or 11 = +/-1,+/-3,+/-4
modulo 13 for PSL2(13). Neither congruence holds. Their absence is not
needed for the conservative cover argument above.

What a supplied certificate must check
-------------------------------------

For three given Original matrices, a strict checker should reconstruct
forty-nine matrices from actual words in precisely those generators and
verify that their flattened forty-nine dimensional vectors are linearly
independent over F_11. This is a sufficient certificate for (i); it
does not assert that arbitrary linear combinations are group elements.
An inverse of the forty-nine by forty-nine word matrix is an alternative
independence certificate; a single verified product AB=I suffices for
square matrices over a field. Every product,
field operation and file read remains payable by the normal resource
owner. Do not reuse a discovery program's independence claim unchecked.

For (ii), check w^24=I, w^12!=I and w^8!=I for an actual reconstructed
word w. These three comparisons prove exact order 24, since 2 and 3
are its only prime divisors. Alternatively, the comparisons w^44=I,
w^22!=I and w^4!=I prove exact order 44, since its prime divisors are
2 and 11.

If H=<r1,r2,r3>=G and r0 is nonidentity, the full rank four intersection
property already fails on the disjoint index sets {0} and {1,2,3}.
Indeed <r0> intersect H=<r0> is nontrivial, whereas the group generated
by the empty intersection of those index sets is the trivial group.
No membership exclusion from <r1,r2> is needed for this argument.
The Original context must still certify that r0 is a nonidentity member
of the intended G. No positive intersection theorem, named proper group
recognition, or full census result follows merely from a successful
discovery of the word list.
